<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The sell-off in chip stocks is deepening t=
oday, worrying investors as markets. News of a rise in US inflation to 3.7%=
 is driving concerns about interest rate hikes, with AI-linked stocks beari=
ng the brunt.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In South Korea, the KOSPI share index has =
plunged by over 8%, extending its recent losses. Chip giants Samsung Electr=
onics (-9%) and SK Hynix (-10.5%) are leading the rout. Japan=E2=80=99s Nik=
kei 225 is sliding too =E2=80=93 it=E2=80=99s down by 4.5% in late trading.=
</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This follows a drop on Wall Street on Wedn=
esday, where the S&amp;P 500 index fell by 1.1% and the Nasdaq Composite lo=
st 1.9%. That includes an 11% plunge in Dell=E2=80=99s shares, which <a hre=
f=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18=
IR3do1js2PLchtDIYBAM8Dz3rwYPdncpV1rrKXDYAXBCVjAzquI1ca9Mr7yJWiZVUIYw3CBl8AY=
_9hqM9ck0EYzT9QxgmnARaXjHwvLp-Yfwj1aDtWn-qUZdTpJLDUT1rKjlYqObg2AphEsMmJg8ZU=
K98Qls3vR3vWX8Lml7PtJXPUlh_XYYXMgPXOQCnGZv3M5vovw5zBcMn9sSo16K2BDtpF3eadQaZ=
XN3Ev0rDJ4dF-qsh98-PuHwu_ljrrdZClw/4uw/c28ImgdPQUmNMA0AeiwdCg/h30/h001.8uhm=
sHCE8XUQ45P_Wm7aMxamd06TzFH-Id6aNL3iY0I" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">CNBC bla=
mes</a> on mounting concerns that enterprise spending on AI won=E2=80=99t j=
ustify the AI buildout.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>The UK economy posted modest growt=
h in August, beating expectations. </strong>The ONS says monthly GDP grew b=
y 0.1% in August, beating expectations of a 0.1% fall. That follows unrevis=
ed growth of 0.4% in July. June=E2=80=99s growth rate has been revised up t=
oo =E2=80=93 to +0.4%, up from +0.3% previously.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">However, emeritus professor Joe Nellis, he=
ad of economic research at accountancy and advisory firm MHA, points out th=
at the underlying pace of growth in the UK economy remains weak.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">August=E2=80=99s growth means the UK econo=
my continues to perform well against international rivals. Britain had alre=
ady grown faster than fellow-G7 members France, Germany, Italy, Japan and C=
anada in the first half of 2026, and roughly as fast as the US.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Jake Finney, senior economist at PwC, says=
: =E2=80=98The latest GDP figures are encouraging, with the economy growing=
 by 0.7% in the three months to August, its strongest growth rate since May=
. On its current trajectory, the UK is likely to record growth comfortably =
above 1% this year, which would probably be enough to make it the second-fa=
stest-growing economy in the G7.=E2=80=99</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.thegua=
rdian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEAX2I5fp6olyh5=
yUeClMls_oO81-uKxH5qD9OgE-OcfN1V2E45Gw6za9diwWwlYdZtvZesieVBYv-UP2JWHQRUjWd=
